#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""container-watch: weekly container update & security-advisory check for the owner's Unraid server.

STRICTLY READ-ONLY on the server. One call of the dispatcher's read-only `containers` verb runs the embedded
COLLECTOR server-side, which only reads: `docker inspect` / `docker image inspect` / `docker history` metadata (through Go templates, so only
names, images, labels, ports and *VERSION= env vars leave the server, never other env/secrets), Unraid's
/var/lib/docker/unraid-update-status.json, the CA Auto Update settings, and one `avahi-browse` mDNS query
(Home Assistant runs in a VM and announces its version there). Nothing is pulled, updated or restarted.
The UniFi OS Server VM (outside Docker): two read-only ssh commands as its unprivileged user (`dpkg-query -W unifi`
inside the uosserver container for the Network app, `uosserver version`), never a write.
`container-watch --show-collector` prints exactly what runs there.
Installed versions come from the app itself where that's a cheap GET (Radarr/Sonarr keys in
~/.secrets/media.json, the paperless token in ~/.secrets/paperless.json, never printed; Seerr, Emby, SABnzbd,
UniFi and FlareSolverr answer without a key), else image labels, *VERSION env vars or binhex RELEASETAG
build args.

Upstream (cached in ~/.cache/container-watch/, 7 days by default, GitHub responses ETag-revalidated):
  GitHub releases + repository security advisories (api.github.com, unauthenticated: 60 req/h, 2 per app),
  NVD CVEs by CPE for apps without GitHub advisories (UniFi Network, Emby, Transmission),
  Ubiquiti's firmware API for the latest UniFi Network version, Docker Hub tags for pinned image tags,
  Ubiquiti's public download list (download.svc.ui.com, no login, GA releases only) for the UniFi OS Server VM's
  Network app (Debian/Ubuntu entry) and UniFi OS Server (Linux x64 entry), NVD for both,
  CISA's Known Exploited Vulnerabilities catalogue (KEV): an affecting CVE listed there is exploited in the wild,
  so it is actionable at once (CRITICAL, no grace days) and re-alerts even if its advisory was already sent.

Bundled runtimes (Node, Bun, Deno, Python, PHP, Ruby, Go, Java inside the images; the app's own version says
nothing about them): versions come from the container's *VERSION env (collector), the image's config blob in its
registry (env + build-arg history; fetched by the local image ID = config digest, one blob GET, no manifest pull;
cached per digest for good) and runtime base-image tags (python:3.11.16-slim, oven/bun:1.4.4). Home Assistant
add-ons run inside the HA VM, outside Docker: their installed versions come from HA's update entities (one
read-only `ha list update --json`) and their images from ADDON_IMAGES. Judged against
  Node: nodejs.org/dist/index.json ("security": true releases on the installed major line) + the CVEs and severity
        of the matching nodejs.org/en/blog/vulnerability post,
  Bun, Deno: their GitHub repository advisories (same rules as apps),
  every runtime: endoflife.date (an end-of-life branch gets no security fixes).
Python/PHP/Ruby/Go/Java behind a release of a security-only branch are listed in --report, never alerted. Findings on
the assistant's own containers (claude-*) go to an inbox note for the assistant, not to the owner's Telegram;
claude-bot (runtimes swapped at boot) and the vault are never judged.

Modes
  container-watch            print only ACTIONABLE items; exit 1 if any, 0 if none, 2 = could not collect.
                             Actionable = one of
                             - an advisory/CVE whose affected range includes the installed version
                               (medium+ with a fix released >= 3 days ago; high/critical even without a fix;
                               any severity at once when the CVE is in CISA's KEV catalogue)
                             - a newer stable release, out >= 3 days, whose notes mention security/CVE/GHSA/
                               vulnerability (CA Auto Update runs daily, so 3 days late means it's stuck)
                             - Unraid has shown "update available" for >= 30 days while >= 3 releases piled up
                             - a bundled runtime that misses a security release of its line (Node), has an
                               affecting advisory (Bun, Deno; same severity/grace rules), or is end-of-life
                             - the UniFi OS Server VM's Network app or OS Server behind the newest stable
                               release (at once, no grace days: nothing updates the VM by itself; once per
                               release)
  container-watch --report   full Markdown table + notes + bundled runtimes
  container-watch --json     everything as JSON
  --notify      text the owner ONE short message (<= 600 chars) about NEW actionable items and leave a
                note in ~/inbox; each item alerts only once (state.json). Cron: Wednesday 19:13. Plain `tg` (out
                at once, also at night) only when a new item is exploited in the wild (KEV) on a container the
                internet reaches through his proxy (its upstream ports / subdomains in the `proxy pull` mirror;
                no mirror = exposed); every other digest goes `tg --soft` (waits out quiet hours and sleep-in).
  container-watch --arr-api [--tag sonarr=TAG] [--tag radarr=TAG] [--json]
                Sonarr v5 / Radarr 7 tripwire (read-only, GitHub only): every /api/v3 call in his arr scripts
                (media, media-remux, arr-unblock, arr-mismatch next to this script; string literals, method = the
                HTTP verb on the line, else GET) vs src/<App>.Api.V3/openapi.json on raw.githubusercontent.com at
                `develop` (the next major; cached 12h) and at each --tag (cached 30 days). Lists per app and ref the
                calls whose path is gone, whose method is gone, or whose query parameters are gone. Exit 0 all
                present, 1 something missing/changed (or no Api.V3 spec at that ref), 2 a spec could not be fetched.
  --dry-run     don't save state and don't send
  --ack         mark everything currently actionable as already alerted, without sending (use after telling
                the owner some other way, so the weekly run doesn't repeat it)
  --max-age D   cache age before refetching, e.g. 7d (default), 1d, 12h. Cron passes 1d so the weekly run
                sees fresh data (GitHub revalidates with ETags; an unchanged 304 doesn't count against the
                limit), while ad-hoc runs reuse the 7-day cache.
  --offline     upstream from cache only (no network except the server and its apps)
  --grace-days N  how old a fix must be before a missing update counts as overdue (default 3)
Files: ~/.local/state/container-watch/  state.json, last-collect.txt, last.json, runs.log, cron.log
Testing: --input FILE (saved collector output, e.g. last-collect.txt; skips ssh and local app APIs),
         --state-dir, --cache-dir, --inbox-dir; env CONTAINER_WATCH_TG (sender, default `tg`), CONTAINER_WATCH_HA
         (the `ha` CLI for the add-on read, default `ha`),
         CONTAINER_WATCH_HOST (ssh alias, default unraid), CONTAINER_WATCH_UNIFI_HOST (ssh alias of the UniFi OS
         VM, default unifi-os; empty = skip the VM), CONTAINER_WATCH_PROXY_SITES (nginx sites dir of the
         proxy mirror). Offline tests: tests/server/test_container_watch*.py (~/opt/assistant).
This tool never updates anything. Updating a container is the owner's call, always with his explicit OK.
"""

